{"date": "2026-10-15", "post_id": 1489307, "title": "The Milky Way over Manhattan", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-15-the-milky-way-over-manhattan/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-november-12-eclipse-in-the-city/\">city lights</a> outshine the stars in the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_City_That_Never_Sleeps_(nickname)\">City That Never Sleeps</a>. Who would have thought that the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-august-2-fireflies-meteors-and-milky-way/\">Milky Way</a> could be visible in this sky? This <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeNUSc7u_KQ/\">image</a> was taken with a filter that blocks most <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible light</a>, capturing instead only light in the near-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/07_infraredwaves/\">infrared</a> region of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/01_intro/\">electromagnetic spectrum</a> (with lower energy than visible light). The astrophotographer's <a href=\"https://petapixel.com/2026/10/07/photographer-captures-the-milky-way-over-manhattan-despite-the-light-pollution/\">clever setup</a> blocks most of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-26-milky-way-urban-style/\">light pollution</a> from the city: old <a href=\"https://scienceinschool.org/article/2015/starlight-inside-light-bulb/\">incandescent light bulbs</a> and some <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ok__l1Acuwg\">neon lights</a> are still <a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Spectrum-of-the-near-infrared-band-of-a-neon-lamp-detected-by-a-commercial-spectrometer_fig2_302915423\">bright</a> in the near-infrared, but <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/spectrum-of-a-fluorescent-light-bulb-vs-spectrum-of-the-sun/\">fluorescent</a> and <a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Emission-spectra-of-different-light-sources-a-incandescent-tungsten-light-bulb-b_fig1_312320039\">LED</a> light bulbs are not. Of course, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stars/\">stars</a> are also bright in the near-infrared! This image is a reminder that we all live <a href=\"https://iauarchive.eso.org/news/announcements/detail/ann20019/\">under the same sky</a>, and astronomy allows all of us to learn more about <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uLu1cTKBspI\">humanity's place in the Cosmos</a>. <br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>Chasing the ISS", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/frozen.spectrum/\">Jason Kurth</a>, <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tmbucksley/?hl=en\">Ty Buckley</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/frozen.spectrum/\">Jason Kurth</a>, <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tmbucksley/?hl=en\">Ty Buckley</a>", "alt": "A black and white view of the Manhattan skyline merges into a night sky with stars and a visible, though faint, Milky Way. Please see explanation for more details.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-15-the-milky-way-over-manhattan/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Manhattan%20Milkyway.jpg?w=2160&h=3840&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 15 - The Milky Way over Manhattan</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-15-the-milky-way-over-manhattan/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Cecilia Chirenti\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: The city lights outshine the stars in the City That Never Sleeps. Who would have thought that the Milky Way could be visible in this sky? This image was taken with a filter that blocks most visible light, capturing instead only light in the near-infrared region of the electromagnetic spectrum (with lower energy than visible light). The astrophotographer&#039;s clever setup blocks most of the light pollution from the city: old incandescent light bulbs and some neon lights are still bright in the near-infrared, but fluorescent and LED light bulbs are not. Of course, stars are also bright in the near-infrared! This image is a reminder that we all live under the same sky, and astronomy allows all of us to learn more about humanity&#039;s place in the Cosmos. Tomorrow&#039;s picture: Chasing the ISS\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"the milky way over manhattan\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The Milky Way over Manhattan\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-15-the-milky-way-over-manhattan/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Manhattan%20Milkyway.jpg?w=2160&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Manhattan%20Milkyway.jpg?w=2160&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A black and white view of the Manhattan skyline merges into a night sky with stars and a visible, though faint, Milky Way. Please see explanation for more details.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-15T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-14T16:25:02-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"The Milky Way over Manhattan\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Manhattan%20Milkyway.jpg?w=2160&#038;h=3840&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 15<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Manhattan%20Milkyway.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/Manhattan%20Milkyway.jpg\" alt=\"A black and white view of the Manhattan skyline merges into a night sky with stars and a visible, though faint, Milky Way. Please see explanation for more details.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> The Milky Way over Manhattan </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/frozen.spectrum/\">Jason Kurth</a>, <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tmbucksley/?hl=en\">Ty Buckley</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-november-12-eclipse-in-the-city/\">city lights</a> outshine the stars in the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_City_That_Never_Sleeps_(nickname)\">City That Never Sleeps</a>. Who would have thought that the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-august-2-fireflies-meteors-and-milky-way/\">Milky Way</a> could be visible in this sky? This <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeNUSc7u_KQ/\">image</a> was taken with a filter that blocks most <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible light</a>, capturing instead only light in the near-<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/07_infraredwaves/\">infrared</a> region of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/01_intro/\">electromagnetic spectrum</a> (with lower energy than visible light). The astrophotographer's <a href=\"https://petapixel.com/2026/10/07/photographer-captures-the-milky-way-over-manhattan-despite-the-light-pollution/\">clever setup</a> blocks most of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-26-milky-way-urban-style/\">light pollution</a> from the city: old <a href=\"https://scienceinschool.org/article/2015/starlight-inside-light-bulb/\">incandescent light bulbs</a> and some <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ok__l1Acuwg\">neon lights</a> are still <a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Spectrum-of-the-near-infrared-band-of-a-neon-lamp-detected-by-a-commercial-spectrometer_fig2_302915423\">bright</a> in the near-infrared, but <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/spectrum-of-a-fluorescent-light-bulb-vs-spectrum-of-the-sun/\">fluorescent</a> and <a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Emission-spectra-of-different-light-sources-a-incandescent-tungsten-light-bulb-b_fig1_312320039\">LED</a> light bulbs are not. Of course, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stars/\">stars</a> are also bright in the near-infrared! This image is a reminder that we all live <a href=\"https://iauarchive.eso.org/news/announcements/detail/ann20019/\">under the same sky</a>, and astronomy allows all of us to learn more about <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uLu1cTKBspI\">humanity's place in the Cosmos</a>. <br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>Chasing the ISS\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-14-mars-and-the-beehive-under-an-aurora/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nCecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261015/html"}